  1. Natural Language Match Querying
Nobody is doing this. After a match is processed, let the manager talk to the match. Not read a report – actually ask questions in plain English and get answers grounded in the video data.
"Show me every time we lost possession in our own half."
"What did number 7 do in the last 10 minutes?"
"Compare our kickout strategy in the first half vs the second half."
"Find me every time we had a 3-on-2 overlap on the left wing."
The LLM has the full structured event data. It can query it instantly, generate charts on the fly, and even jump the video player to the relevant timestamp. This turns your analytics platform from something you read into something you interrogate. Managers don't think in dashboards, they think in questions. Let them ask.

5. Player Development Trajectories
Over a season, track not just what a player did but how they're developing. The LLM analyses the arc.
"Dylan's shooting accuracy has improved from 55% to 75% over 8 matches. His shot selection has changed – he's taking fewer long-range shots and getting into better positions inside the 21. His movement off the ball has measurably improved based on the zones he's receiving possession in."
Turn this into a personal development report for each player, generated monthly. Players see their own growth visualised. Parents of underage players see their child's progression. This is retention gold for your platform – people don't cancel a subscription that's showing them they're getting better.
6. Video Clip Auto-Generation
When Gemini analyses the match video and tags events with timestamps, you already have the data to auto-generate highlight clips. FFmpeg can cut clips programmatically.
"Here are Dylan's 4 scoring moments this match" – four 15-second clips, auto-cut, ready to share. Players share these on Instagram, WhatsApp groups, club social media. Every shared clip is free marketing for your platform. Every clip has a subtle OneStat watermark.
You could even auto-generate a 2-minute match highlights reel ranked by event significance (goals first, then points, then key defensive moments). The LLM decides the narrative order. FFmpeg stitches it. The club has a highlights package without anyone editing video.
7. Training Session Design
This is the bridge between match analytics and the training ground. After analysing 3-4 matches, the LLM has identified weaknesses. Now it suggests specific training drills.
"Your kickout win rate is 45%. Here are 3 kickout drills focused on movement patterns and communication, specifically targeting the short kickout to the left where you're being pressed."
"Your team concedes 60% of scores from turnovers in your own half-forward line. Here's a drill focused on decision-making under pressure in that zone, emphasising when to go short vs kick long."
Integrate with your Training section in the nav bar. The match analysis directly feeds training recommendations. That closes the loop between analysis and action, which is where most analytics platforms stop.


8. The Commentary Track (very nice to have - not important)
This one is almost whimsical but could be brilliant for engagement. After processing a match, generate an AI commentary track that can be played over the video. The LLM has the timestamps, the events, the context. It can narrate the match like a commentator.
"And Dungloe work it out from the back, number 5 finds number 8 on the wing, he solos past his man, lovely ball inside to number 14 who turns and... POINT! What a score, that's his third from play today and Dungloe lead by two."
Players would absolutely watch their own matches with AI commentary. It's fun, it's shareable, and it demonstrates the depth of your analysis in the most accessible way possible.
The one that changes everything though is number 4 – real-time tactical suggestions. That's the feature that would make managers feel like they have an analyst on the sideline. And you're closer to it than you think, because your manual tagging tool is already generating the real-time event stream you'd need.


